    "result_text": "You can now write off an invoice as bad debt. It's kept on record but no longer counts toward what you're owed, and each write-off is written to the audit log. `bin/e2e` passes.\n\n**How it works**\n- On a sent or part-paid invoice, a **Write off as bad debt** button (`invoice-write-off`) changes its status to `WRITTEN_OFF`. If it fails, an error message appears (`invoice-write-off-error`).\n- The audit log gets a line like `INVOICE_WRITTEN_OFF id=1 amount=100.00`.\n- A written-off invoice is left out of the dashboard total, client balances and client statements, the same way a cancelled (VOID) invoice is.\n- The server refuses new payments or credit notes against a written-off invoice. The credit-note form is hidden for it.\n- A database migration (`V56__invoice_write_off.sql`) adds `WRITTEN_OFF` as an allowed invoice status, and there's a new endpoint `POST /api/projects/{projectId}/invoices/{invoiceId}/write-off`.\n\n**Decisions for you**\n- **Dashboard showing $0.00.** The dashboard normally hides a currency once nothing is owed in it, but the test expects `$0.00` for USD after the write-off. I made a currency stay listed at $0.00 only when it has written-off invoices, so \"Nothing owed\" behaves as before everywhere else. If you'd rather any currency with billing history stay listed, that's a small change, but it would alter what the dashboard shows after an invoice is paid in full.\n- **Audit amount.** The audit line records the balance still owed when the invoice is written off (total minus payments and credits), not the invoice total. In the test these are both $100.00. On a part-paid invoice they differ, so tell me if you'd prefer the invoice total, which is what the VOID audit line uses.\n- **Project invoice total.** A written-off invoice still counts in the project's invoiced total, because it was billed even though it won't be collected.\n\nThe task mentioned an OfficeFloor server, but this app's server is Spring Boot, so I followed the existing Spring code and CLAUDE.md.",
